{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016A3paYBGU2svgEegw6GCBQ","type":"tool_result","content":"//! \"Play in VLC\": `src/main/external-player.js` and the `vlc-command`\n//! package it depends on.\n//!\n//! The Electron module is three exported functions -- `checkInstall`,\n//! `spawn` and `kill` -- over one module-level `proc`, and everything\n//! interesting about it is what the child's life dispatches back at the\n//! renderer:\n//!\n//! - still up after a second: `exitModal`, which takes the\n//!   unsupported-media modal off the screen now that something else is\n//!   playing the video;\n//! - a clean exit: `backToList`, because the user closed the player;\n//! - any other exit, or no player to run at all:\n//!   `externalPlayerNotFound`, which is the message in that same modal;\n//! - killed by us: nothing, because the renderer is the one that asked.\n//!\n//! `vlc-command` is the other half. It is a list of places VLC lives on\n//! each platform, probed with `--version`, and the registry on Windows;\n//! it is not a package any more (US-053 took it out of `package.json`),\n//! so the list is [`vlc_candidates`] here.\n//!\n//! The split follows `paths.rs` and `window.rs`: every decision is a\n//! pure function of plain values -- [`configured`], [`player_program`],\n//! [`vlc_args`], [`plan`] -- so all three platforms' answers are\n//! asserted from a `cargo test` run on any one of them, and\n//! [`ExternalPlayer`] is the only part that makes a process. What the\n//! *host* does around the spawn -- disabling the Playback menu and\n//! handing the media keys back -- is `main.rs`'s, because it is\n//! `src/main/ipc.js`'s rather than this module's.\n\n    }\n}\n\n/// Whatever can answer \"where is VLC\". The real one is [`Lookup`].\npub trait Vlc: Send + Sync {\n    fn find(&self) -> Option<Launch>;\n}\n\n/// `vlc-command`: probe each candidate with `--version` and take the\n/// first that answers, or read the registry on Windows.\n///\n/// The package probes all the candidates at once and keeps the\n/// lowest-indexed one that succeeded; probing them in order and\n/// stopping at the first is the same answer for fewer processes.\npub struct Lookup {\n    platform: Platform,\n    home: Option<PathBuf>,\n}\n\nimpl Lookup {\n    /// The lookup for the machine this is running on.\n    pub fn current() -> Lookup {\n        Lookup {\n            platform: Platform::current(),\n            home: std::env::var_os(\"HOME\").map(PathBuf::from),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Vlc for Lookup {\n    fn find(&self) -> Option<Launch> {\n        if self.platform == Platform::Windows {\n            return VLC_REGISTRY_KEYS\n                .iter()\n                .find_map(|key| registry_install_dir(key))\n                .map(Launch::program);\n        }\n        vlc_candidates(self.platform, self.home.as_deref())\n            .into_iter()\n            .find(answers_version)\n    }\n}\n\n/// Whether a candidate is really there: `cp.exec(cmd + ' --version')`.\nfn answers_version(candidate: &Launch) -> bool {\n    Command::new(&candidate.program)\n        .args(&candidate.args)\n        .arg(\"--version\")\n        .stdin(Stdio::null())\n        .stdout(Stdio::null())\n        .stderr(Stdio::null())\n        .status()\n        .map(|status| status.success())\n        .unwrap_or(false)\n}\n\n/// One `reg query`, parsed.\nfn registry_install_dir(key: &str) -> Option<PathBuf> {\n    let output = Command::new(\"reg\")\n        .args(registry_query(key))\n        .output()\n            .lock()\n            .unwrap_or_else(|err| err.into_inner())\n    }\n}\n\n/// `proc.kill('SIGKILL')`, and then the reaping Node does for us.\nfn end(mut child: Child) {\n    let _ = child.kill();\n    let _ = child.wait();\n}\n\n/// Watch one child: close the modal if it survives, report how it ended\n/// if it was still ours when it did.\nfn watch<F>(shared: Arc<Shared>, generation: u64, dispatch: F)\nwhere\n    F: Fn(&'static str),\n{\n    let deadline = Instant::now() + shared.modal_delay;\n    let mut announced = false;\n\n    loop {\n        let exited = {\n            let mut state = shared.state.lock().unwrap_or_else(|err| err.into_inner());\n            if state.generation != generation {\n                return;\n            }\n            match state.child.as_mut() {\n                // Killed between the two locks.\n                None => return,\n                Some(child) => match child.try_wait() {\n                    Ok(Some(status)) => {\n                        let _ = child.wait();\n                        state.child = None;\n                        Some(status.success())\n                    }\n                    Ok(None) => None,\n                    Err(err) => {\n                        eprintln!(\"external player: could not wait for it: {err}\");\n                        state.child = None;\n                        Some(false)\n                    }\n                },\n            }\n        };\n\n        // The exit is checked before the deadline, so a player that\n        // fails inside the first second reports the failure and never\n        // takes the modal down -- `clearTimeout(closeModalTimeout)`.\n        if let Some(clean) = exited {\n            dispatch(if clean { BACK_TO_LIST } else { NOT_FOUND });\n            return;\n        }\n\n        if !announced && Instant::now() >= deadline {\n            dispatch(EXIT_MODAL);\n            announced = true;\n        }\n        thread::sleep(POLL);\n    }\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n    use std::fs;\n    use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU32, Ordering};\n    // `node` is already what `engine.rs`'s tests and `paths.rs` shell\n    // out to.\n\n    static SEQ: AtomicU32 = AtomicU32::new(0);\n\n    struct Sandbox {\n        dir: PathBuf,\n    }\n\n    impl Sandbox {\n        fn new(name: &str) -> Sandbox {\n            let n = SEQ.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);\n            let dir = std::env::temp_dir().join(format!(\n                \"wt-player-{}-{}-{}\",\n                std::process::id(),\n                n,\n                name\n            ));\n            fs::create_dir_all(&dir).unwrap();\n            Sandbox { dir }\n        }\n\n        fn path(&self, name: &str) -> PathBuf {\n            self.dir.join(name)\n        }\n\n        /// A fake player: it records the arguments it was given, then\n        /// does whatever `body` says.\n        fn player(&self, body: &str) -> PathBuf {\n            let argv = self.path(\"argv.json\");\n            let script = self.path(\"player.js\");\n            let source = format!(\n                r#\"\n                require('fs').writeFileSync({argv:?}, JSON.stringify(process.argv.slice(2)))\n                {body}\n                \"#\n            );\n            fs::write(&script, source).unwrap();\n            script\n        }\n\n        fn argv(&self) -> Vec<String> {\n            let raw = fs::read_to_string(self.path(\"argv.json\")).unwrap_or_default();\n            serde_json::from_str(&raw).unwrap_or_default()\n        }\n    }\n\n    impl Drop for Sandbox {\n        fn drop(&mut self) {\n            let _ = fs::remove_dir_all(&self.dir);\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// A player that never exits on its own.\n    const STAYS_UP: &str = \"setInterval(() => {}, 1000)\";\n\n    /// A lookup that answers with a script under `node`, so that the\n    /// whole VLC branch -- arguments included -- runs against a process\n    /// this test can read.\n    struct Fake(Option<Launch>);\n\n    impl Vlc for Fake {\n        fn find(&self) -> Option<Launch> {\n            self.0.clone()\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn found(script: &Path) -> Box<dyn Vlc> {\n        Box::new(Fake(Some(Launch {\n            program: PathBuf::from(\"node\"),\n            args: vec![script.to_string_lossy().into_owned()],\n        })))\n    }\n\n    fn missing() -> Box<dyn Vlc> {\n        Box::new(Fake(None))\n    }\n\n    fn player(vlc: Box<dyn Vlc>) -> ExternalPlayer {\n        // Short enough that a test can wait for it, long enough that a\n        // player which exits at once loses the race on purpose -- Node\n        // takes tens of milliseconds to start, so the margin has to\n        // survive a loaded machine.\n        ExternalPlayer::new(vlc, Platform::current(), Duration::from_millis(400))\n    }\n\n    /// What the renderer heard.\n    #[derive(Clone, Default)]\n    struct Dispatched(Arc<Mutex<Vec<&'static str>>>);\n\n    impl Dispatched {\n        fn sink(&self) -> impl Fn(&'static str) + Send + 'static {\n            let seen = Arc::clone(&self.0);\n            move |action| seen.lock().unwrap().push(action)\n        }\n\n        fn seen(&self) -> Vec<&'static str> {\n            self.0.lock().unwrap().clone()\n        }\n\n        /// Wait for `action`, or give up. Returns everything heard.\n        fn wait_for(&self, action: &str, timeout: Duration) -> Vec<&'static str> {\n            let deadline = Instant::now() + timeout;\n            loop {\n                let seen = self.seen();\n                if seen.contains(&action) || Instant::now() >= deadline {\n                    return seen;\n                }\n                thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(10));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn a_player_that_stays_up_takes_the_modal_down() {\n        player.kill();\n    }\n\n    /// Whether a pid is still a live process, without a signal crate --\n    /// `engine.rs`'s test helper, for the same reason.\n    #[cfg(unix)]\n    fn is_alive(pid: u32) -> bool {\n        Command::new(\"kill\")\n            .args([\"-0\", &pid.to_string()])\n            .stdout(Stdio::null())\n            .stderr(Stdio::null())\n            .status()\n            .map(|status| status.success())\n            .unwrap_or(false)\n    }\n\n    #[cfg(not(unix))]\n    fn is_alive(_pid: u32) -> bool {\n        false\n    }\n\n    // -- the shim's half ------------------------------------------------\n\nwork) and a real Cmd-Q through `osascript`.\n\n## The external player is a child nobody reaps\n\n`src/external_player.rs` is `src/main/external-player.js` plus the\n`vlc-command` package, which is not a dependency any more. Three things\nabout it are not local to the file:\n\n- **A spawned player outlives the host, on purpose.** Nothing kills it\n  on `RunEvent::Exit` -- Electron leaves it running too, and a check\n  confirmed VLC still playing after the host was killed. The only kill\n  is `quit_external_player`, and it is silent: the renderer asked, so\n  the renderer is not told.\n- **`check_for_external_player` and `open_external_player` are\n  `async`.** The lookup runs a `--version` probe per candidate, each\n  one a process, and `open` also hands the media keys back, which\n  deadlocks from a synchronous command (see the global-shortcut entry\n  below). Both do their blocking on `spawn_blocking`.\n- **`vlc-command`'s Linux list contains a command line, not a path.**\n  `flatpak run org.videolan.VLC` is spawned here as a program plus two\n  arguments; spawning the whole string, as the JS does, is an `ENOENT`\n  upstream that only the shell-backed `--version` probe hid. Only that\n  one entry goes through `Launch::parse`: splitting the rest on\n  whitespace would turn a home directory with a space in it into a\n  program and an argument.\n\n## Driving a real player, or anything that needs a stream\n\nruns -- a launch that finds a ghost exits silently with status 0, and\nthat looks exactly like a probe that did not print.\n\n## Gates\n\n`cargo fmt --check` and `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` are gates from\nUS-051 onward, as is `cargo test`. Clippy recompiles the whole\ndependency tree the first time (several minutes); budget for it rather\nthan assuming it hung.\n\n## Observing the webview non-interactively","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb328e1e-7d30-4abb-b61c-f9c4b744f300","uuid":"411ed972-93fd-4552-ae2b-683ccc38964f","timestamp":"2026-10-18T01:13:38.624Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"//! \"Play in VLC\": `src/main/external-player.js` and the `vlc-command`\n//! package it depends on.\n//!\n//! The Electron module is three exported functions -- `checkInstall`,\n//! `spawn` and `kill` -- over one module-level `proc`, and everything\n//! interesting about it is what the child's life dispatches back at the\n//! renderer:\n//!\n//! - still up after a second: `exitModal`, which takes the\n//!   unsupported-media modal off the screen now that something else is\n//!   playing the video;\n//! - a clean exit: `backToList`, because the user closed the player;\n//! - any other exit, or no player to run at all:\n//!   `externalPlayerNotFound`, which is the message in that same modal;\n//! - killed by us: nothing, because the renderer is the one that asked.\n//!\n//! `vlc-command` is the other half. It is a list of places VLC lives on\n//! each platform, probed with `--version`, and the registry on Windows;\n//! it is not a package any more (US-053 took it out of `package.json`),\n//! so the list is [`vlc_candidates`] here.\n//!\n//! The split follows `paths.rs` and `window.rs`: every decision is a\n//! pure function of plain values -- [`configured`], [`player_program`],\n//! [`vlc_args`], [`plan`] -- so all three platforms' answers are\n//! asserted from a `cargo test` run on any one of them, and\n//! [`ExternalPlayer`] is the only part that makes a process. What the\n//! *host* does around the spawn -- disabling the Playback menu and\n//! handing the media keys back -- is `main.rs`'s, because it is\n//! `src/main/ipc.js`'s rather than this module's.\n\n    }\n}\n\n/// Whatever can answer \"where is VLC\". The real one is [`Lookup`].\npub trait Vlc: Send + Sync {\n    fn find(&self) -> Option<Launch>;\n}\n\n/// `vlc-command`: probe each candidate with `--version` and take the\n/// first that answers, or read the registry on Windows.\n///\n/// The package probes all the candidates at once and keeps the\n/// lowest-indexed one that succeeded; probing them in order and\n/// stopping at the first is the same answer for fewer processes.\npub struct Lookup {\n    platform: Platform,\n    home: Option<PathBuf>,\n}\n\nimpl Lookup {\n    /// The lookup for the machine this is running on.\n    pub fn current() -> Lookup {\n        Lookup {\n            platform: Platform::current(),\n            home: std::env::var_os(\"HOME\").map(PathBuf::from),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Vlc for Lookup {\n    fn find(&self) -> Option<Launch> {\n        if self.platform == Platform::Windows {\n            return VLC_REGISTRY_KEYS\n                .iter()\n                .find_map(|key| registry_install_dir(key))\n                .map(Launch::program);\n        }\n        vlc_candidates(self.platform, self.home.as_deref())\n            .into_iter()\n            .find(answers_version)\n    }\n}\n\n/// Whether a candidate is really there: `cp.exec(cmd + ' --version')`.\nfn answers_version(candidate: &Launch) -> bool {\n    Command::new(&candidate.program)\n        .args(&candidate.args)\n        .arg(\"--version\")\n        .stdin(Stdio::null())\n        .stdout(Stdio::null())\n        .stderr(Stdio::null())\n        .status()\n        .map(|status| status.success())\n        .unwrap_or(false)\n}\n\n/// One `reg query`, parsed.\nfn registry_install_dir(key: &str) -> Option<PathBuf> {\n    let output = Command::new(\"reg\")\n        .args(registry_query(key))\n        .output()\n            .lock()\n            .unwrap_or_else(|err| err.into_inner())\n    }\n}\n\n/// `proc.kill('SIGKILL')`, and then the reaping Node does for us.\nfn end(mut child: Child) {\n    let _ = child.kill();\n    let _ = child.wait();\n}\n\n/// Watch one child: close the modal if it survives, report how it ended\n/// if it was still ours when it did.\nfn watch<F>(shared: Arc<Shared>, generation: u64, dispatch: F)\nwhere\n    F: Fn(&'static str),\n{\n    let deadline = Instant::now() + shared.modal_delay;\n    let mut announced = false;\n\n    loop {\n        let exited = {\n            let mut state = shared.state.lock().unwrap_or_else(|err| err.into_inner());\n            if state.generation != generation {\n                return;\n            }\n            match state.child.as_mut() {\n                // Killed between the two locks.\n                None => return,\n                Some(child) => match child.try_wait() {\n                    Ok(Some(status)) => {\n                        let _ = child.wait();\n                        state.child = None;\n                        Some(status.success())\n                    }\n                    Ok(None) => None,\n                    Err(err) => {\n                        eprintln!(\"external player: could not wait for it: {err}\");\n                        state.child = None;\n                        Some(false)\n                    }\n                },\n            }\n        };\n\n        // The exit is checked before the deadline, so a player that\n        // fails inside the first second reports the failure and never\n        // takes the modal down -- `clearTimeout(closeModalTimeout)`.\n        if let Some(clean) = exited {\n            dispatch(if clean { BACK_TO_LIST } else { NOT_FOUND });\n            return;\n        }\n\n        if !announced && Instant::now() >= deadline {\n            dispatch(EXIT_MODAL);\n            announced = true;\n        }\n        thread::sleep(POLL);\n    }\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n    use std::fs;\n    use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU32, Ordering};\n    // `node` is already what `engine.rs`'s tests and `paths.rs` shell\n    // out to.\n\n    static SEQ: AtomicU32 = AtomicU32::new(0);\n\n    struct Sandbox {\n        dir: PathBuf,\n    }\n\n    impl Sandbox {\n        fn new(name: &str) -> Sandbox {\n            let n = SEQ.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);\n            let dir = std::env::temp_dir().join(format!(\n                \"wt-player-{}-{}-{}\",\n                std::process::id(),\n                n,\n                name\n            ));\n            fs::create_dir_all(&dir).unwrap();\n            Sandbox { dir }\n        }\n\n        fn path(&self, name: &str) -> PathBuf {\n            self.dir.join(name)\n        }\n\n        /// A fake player: it records the arguments it was given, then\n        /// does whatever `body` says.\n        fn player(&self, body: &str) -> PathBuf {\n            let argv = self.path(\"argv.json\");\n            let script = self.path(\"player.js\");\n            let source = format!(\n                r#\"\n                require('fs').writeFileSync({argv:?}, JSON.stringify(process.argv.slice(2)))\n                {body}\n                \"#\n            );\n            fs::write(&script, source).unwrap();\n            script\n        }\n\n        fn argv(&self) -> Vec<String> {\n            let raw = fs::read_to_string(self.path(\"argv.json\")).unwrap_or_default();\n            serde_json::from_str(&raw).unwrap_or_default()\n        }\n    }\n\n    impl Drop for Sandbox {\n        fn drop(&mut self) {\n            let _ = fs::remove_dir_all(&self.dir);\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// A player that never exits on its own.\n    const STAYS_UP: &str = \"setInterval(() => {}, 1000)\";\n\n    /// A lookup that answers with a script under `node`, so that the\n    /// whole VLC branch -- arguments included -- runs against a process\n    /// this test can read.\n    struct Fake(Option<Launch>);\n\n    impl Vlc for Fake {\n        fn find(&self) -> Option<Launch> {\n            self.0.clone()\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn found(script: &Path) -> Box<dyn Vlc> {\n        Box::new(Fake(Some(Launch {\n            program: PathBuf::from(\"node\"),\n            args: vec![script.to_string_lossy().into_owned()],\n        })))\n    }\n\n    fn missing() -> Box<dyn Vlc> {\n        Box::new(Fake(None))\n    }\n\n    fn player(vlc: Box<dyn Vlc>) -> ExternalPlayer {\n        // Short enough that a test can wait for it, long enough that a\n        // player which exits at once loses the race on purpose -- Node\n        // takes tens of milliseconds to start, so the margin has to\n        // survive a loaded machine.\n        ExternalPlayer::new(vlc, Platform::current(), Duration::from_millis(400))\n    }\n\n    /// What the renderer heard.\n    #[derive(Clone, Default)]\n    struct Dispatched(Arc<Mutex<Vec<&'static str>>>);\n\n    impl Dispatched {\n        fn sink(&self) -> impl Fn(&'static str) + Send + 'static {\n            let seen = Arc::clone(&self.0);\n            move |action| seen.lock().unwrap().push(action)\n        }\n\n        fn seen(&self) -> Vec<&'static str> {\n            self.0.lock().unwrap().clone()\n        }\n\n        /// Wait for `action`, or give up. Returns everything heard.\n        fn wait_for(&self, action: &str, timeout: Duration) -> Vec<&'static str> {\n            let deadline = Instant::now() + timeout;\n            loop {\n                let seen = self.seen();\n                if seen.contains(&action) || Instant::now() >= deadline {\n                    return seen;\n                }\n                thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(10));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn a_player_that_stays_up_takes_the_modal_down() {\n        player.kill();\n    }\n\n    /// Whether a pid is still a live process, without a signal crate --\n    /// `engine.rs`'s test helper, for the same reason.\n    #[cfg(unix)]\n    fn is_alive(pid: u32) -> bool {\n        Command::new(\"kill\")\n            .args([\"-0\", &pid.to_string()])\n            .stdout(Stdio::null())\n            .stderr(Stdio::null())\n            .status()\n            .map(|status| status.success())\n            .unwrap_or(false)\n    }\n\n    #[cfg(not(unix))]\n    fn is_alive(_pid: u32) -> bool {\n        false\n    }\n\n    // -- the shim's half ------------------------------------------------\n\nwork) and a real Cmd-Q through `osascript`.\n\n## The external player is a child nobody reaps\n\n`src/external_player.rs` is `src/main/external-player.js` plus the\n`vlc-command` package, which is not a dependency any more. Three things\nabout it are not local to the file:\n\n- **A spawned player outlives the host, on purpose.** Nothing kills it\n  on `RunEvent::Exit` -- Electron leaves it running too, and a check\n  confirmed VLC still playing after the host was killed. The only kill\n  is `quit_external_player`, and it is silent: the renderer asked, so\n  the renderer is not told.\n- **`check_for_external_player` and `open_external_player` are\n  `async`.** The lookup runs a `--version` probe per candidate, each\n  one a process, and `open` also hands the media keys back, which\n  deadlocks from a synchronous command (see the global-shortcut entry\n  below). Both do their blocking on `spawn_blocking`.\n- **`vlc-command`'s Linux list contains a command line, not a path.**\n  `flatpak run org.videolan.VLC` is spawned here as a program plus two\n  arguments; spawning the whole string, as the JS does, is an `ENOENT`\n  upstream that only the shell-backed `--version` probe hid. Only that\n  one entry goes through `Launch::parse`: splitting the rest on\n  whitespace would turn a home directory with a space in it into a\n  program and an argument.\n\n## Driving a real player, or anything that needs a stream\n\nruns -- a launch that finds a ghost exits silently with status 0, and\nthat looks exactly like a probe that did not print.\n\n## Gates\n\n`cargo fmt --check` and `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` are gates from\nUS-051 onward, as is `cargo test`. Clippy recompiles the whole\ndependency tree the first time (several minutes); budget for it rather\nthan assuming it hung.\n\n## Observing the webview non-interactively","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016A3paYBGU2svgEegw6GCBQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
